# Vercel deployment ignore file
# This file excludes files and directories from Vercel deployment
# to stay under the 15,000 file limit

# Backup directories (major culprits)
backup/
backup_logs/
starcom-mk2-backup/

# ─── NOT WEB BUILD INPUTS, AND VERY LARGE ──────────────────────────────────
#
# A deploy was measured uploading 609MB. `desktop/` alone is 1.088GB, of which
# desktop/release is 637MB of BUILT DESKTOP BINARIES — uploaded on every deploy,
# for a web build that references none of it (checked against vite.config.ts and
# package.json). node_modules inside it was already excluded by the pattern
# below; the release output never was.
#
# This is the upload half of the deployment-size problem. The other half — what
# each deployment STORES — is minify-dist-json.mjs and the asset work in the
# same series.
#
# If any of these ever becomes a build input, guard-vercel-build-inputs fails
# the build rather than letting the deploy die on MODULE_NOT_FOUND. That guard
# is why excluding aggressively here is safe.
# Anchored with a leading slash. Unanchored, `tools/` matches a directory of
# that name at ANY depth — it excluded src/applications/netrunner/tools and the
# build failed to resolve NetRunnerPowerTools. guard-vercel-build-inputs did not
# catch it because it checks the files the build CHAIN runs, not what src
# imports.
/desktop/
/archives/
/tools/

# ─── MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The upload was measured at 1076MB before these rules and 202MB after the
# three above. These three are what was left, and none is a web build input:
#
#   .venv     76MB  a Python virtualenv
#   .vercel   36MB  the CLI's own local state; .vercel/output is 36MB of a
#                   PREVIOUS build's output. project.json is read off disk by
#                   the CLI before it uploads anything, so excluding it from
#                   the upload costs nothing.
#   .generated 18MB baked territory artifacts, written by
#                   scripts/geopolitical/bake-territories.ts and read by
#                   nothing in the build — the shipped copies live in
#                   public/geopolitical.
#
# What remains is public/ (52MB of assets that must ship) and src/ (17MB).
/.venv/
/.vercel/
/.generated/

# Node modules (should be excluded anyway)
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Build outputs and dependencies
target/
dist/
dist-ssr/
build/
out/

# Development and test files
#
# ─── ANCHORED, FOR THE THIRD TIME ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `coverage/` was unanchored and matched src/services/intel/coverage, so the
# deploy died on "Could not resolve ../../../services/intel/coverage/coverage"
# — three production builds in a row, while the last good one stayed live and
# the site served an ever-staler version of itself.
#
# This is the same mistake as `tools/` above, which excluded
# src/applications/netrunner/tools, and the same as the parent-exclusion block
# further down. Unanchored, a directory pattern matches at ANY depth, and this
# repository keeps a `coverage`, a `tests` and a `contracts` directory inside
# src/ that are nothing to do with the ones meant here.
#
# `tests/` and `contracts/` were not breaking anything yet — nothing in src
# imports across them today — and they are anchored anyway, because "not
# imported yet" is a property of this afternoon rather than of the pattern.
test-results/
/tests/
playwright-report/
/coverage/
.cache/
.npm/
.yarn/

# Documentation and scripts (not needed for deployment)
# `docs/*` used to be here, and it is why four build steps failed in a row.
#
# Excluding the PARENT means `!docs/spec/**` below cannot bring the specs back:
# git documents that a re-include cannot reach inside an excluded directory, and
# the deploy export behaved that way — docs/spec/vectors/index.json was absent,
# which is not a .md and so was excluded by the directory rule alone.
# guard-spec-drift, guard-hardcoded-relays and conformance all read from
# docs/spec, and publish-spec.mjs SERVES it later in the same chain.
#
# So the parent is not excluded any more. `*.md` above already removes the bulk
# of docs (16MB of markdown), and docs/assets is 48MB of the remaining 48MB.
# What is left of docs is under a megabyte, and docs/spec arrives intact without
# depending on re-include semantics that differ between implementations.
docs/assets/
# The decision records and the samples kept beside them, read by tests and probes, never by the build
# (checked 2026-10-13: no build-chain script or app module reads them). Uploaded, a push of a sample alone
# was a build: the Ignored Build Step (scripts/vercel-build-input.mjs) saw `docs` change.
/docs/missions/
# Restrict ignoring of build/utility scripts to the root-level scripts directory only.
# `/scripts/*` used to be here, with fifteen negations below trying to undo it.
#
# It is gone, and the negations with it. The whole arrangement existed to save
# 2.2MB — the entire scripts directory — while `desktop/` shipped 1GB of built
# binaries that nobody had excluded. That is the wrong trade twice over.
#
# It was also actively breaking. Excluding the PARENT means a negation cannot
# reach inside it: `!/scripts/peer/**` did not bring scripts/peer back, and the
# deploy died on MODULE_NOT_FOUND for scripts/peer/peer.mjs — the same failure
# the block below records costing five months of deploys, returning by the same
# mechanism. docs/spec was absent for the identical reason.
#
# Only the tests are excluded now, which no build step runs.
scripts/__tests__/
# Ensure NetRunner engine "scripts" subtree (nested) is retained (overrides broader patterns just in case)
!src/applications/netrunner/scripts/
!src/applications/netrunner/scripts/engine/
!src/applications/netrunner/scripts/engine/**
*.sh
*.md
!README.md

# Asset development files (exclude development assets, keep production ones)
asset development/

# Exclude asset files from development directories only
asset development/**/*.psd
asset development/**/*.ai
asset development/**/*.svg
asset development/**/*.png
asset development/**/*.jpg
asset development/**/*.jpeg
asset development/**/*.gif
asset development/**/*.ico
asset development/**/*.glb

# Exclude root-level asset files (not in public or src directories)
/*.svg
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg
/*.gif
/*.ico
/*.glb

# Configuration files that aren't needed for deployment
*.config.js
*.config.ts
*.config.cjs
*.config.mjs
!vite.config.*
!next.config.*
!tailwind.config.*
!postcss.config.*
# Keep all TypeScript config files - they're needed for Vite build
# tsconfig.*.json
# !tsconfig.json
eslint.config.js
vitest.config.ts

# Log files
*.log
*.log.*
backup_verify_log.txt

# Development database files
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
/data/
!src/data/
!src/data/rlshEmbeddedDataset.ts
!src/data/rlshCuratedRegistration.ts
!src/data/rlshRoles/
!src/data/rlshRoles/**

# Git files
.git/
.gitignore
.gitmodules

# IDE files
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.tmp
*.temp

# Environment files (should be configured in Vercel dashboard)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Rust specific
Cargo.lock
**/*.rs.bk

# Contracts (if not needed for frontend). Anchored: src/contracts exists.
/contracts/

# Any other large asset directories (exclude only asset model directories, not src/models)
/models/
public/models/*.glb
public/models/*.gltf
asset development/models/
stock/

# ── Build-time requirements — MUST reach the build container ─────────────
#
# These negations sit last on purpose: .vercelignore is gitignore-shaped, so
# the final matching pattern wins, and the broad `*.md` and `docs/` rules above
# would otherwise re-exclude them.
#
# This block exists because omitting it cost five months of deploys. `/scripts/`
# was excluded while package.json's build ran `tsx scripts/verify-netrunner-
# engine.ts`, so every build died with MODULE_NOT_FOUND before reaching vite —
# and the last successful deploy stayed live from March to August while `main`
# moved 156 commits ahead of it. Nothing reported this: the site kept serving,
# just an ever-staler version of itself.
#
# If you add a step to the build chain, add its file here in the same commit.

# Every .mjs in the root scripts directory, as a PATTERN rather than a list.
#
# The list this replaces held eight names while the build chain needed nineteen,
# so eleven build steps were being stripped from the container — including the
# very first one, which is why the deploy died before reaching vite. The block
# above says "if you add a step to the build chain, add its file here in the
# same commit"; that instruction was followed zero times out of eleven,
# including by the commit that added guard-native-capabilities.mjs.
#
# An instruction obeyed nobody is a design problem, not a discipline problem. A
# pattern cannot be forgotten, and scripts/guard-vercel-build-inputs.mjs now
# fails the build locally if a build step would not survive this file.

# And every .ts in that directory, for the same reason and by the same argument.
#
# The pattern above was applied to .mjs and the .ts files were listed by name
# instead — so scripts/make-vectors.ts, which nothing in the build chain runs,
# was stripped from the container. That is not a missing build step; it is a
# missing CONSUMER. guard-orphan-exports scans the tree it is given, found
# contentDigest with no caller left, and correctly failed the build for an
# orphan that does not exist in the repository.
#
# A guard that reads the source tree answers differently when the tree is
# truncated, so the tree the guards see must be the tree that exists. A pattern
# cannot be forgotten; a list of four names already was.

# Guards that now run IN the build, because CI does not run at all — 79 startup
# failures to 2 successes, the same billing signature two peer repos hit. Every
# guard in this repository was CI-only and had therefore never executed once.
# Added here in the same commit as the build-chain change, per the rule above.

# The published specification and its conformance vectors. publish-spec.mjs
# copies these into the served output so peers can fetch what they build
# against; four other nodes depend on them being retrievable.
!docs/spec/
!docs/spec/**

# ── Served documents that are NOT regenerated at build time ──────────────
#
# `public/` is by definition what the site serves. The broad `*.md` rule near
# the top is aimed at repository documentation, but it matches there too, and
# the result is a document that exists in the tree, passes review, deploys
# without error, and 404s at the URL it is published under.
#
# It went unnoticed because public/spec/*.md serves fine — those are written by
# publish-spec.mjs DURING the build, after this filter has already been applied,
# so the only markdown anyone checked was the markdown that regenerates itself.
# security-policy.md is hand-authored, and it is the document security.txt sends
# a journalist or a relay operator to. It was returning 404 while security.txt,
# in the same directory, served correctly.
#
# A pattern rather than a filename, for the reason the scripts block above
# records: that list held eight names while nineteen were needed.
!public/**/*.md
